WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
Elon Musk runs not one but two incredible&nbsp;
companies: Tesla and SpaceX. But what if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.800
told you that he doesn’t actually run SpaceX&nbsp;
day-to-day, and I have the data to prove it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:14.080
The flight logs from Elon’s private jet make&nbsp;
it clear that Tesla is his number one priority,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:18.000
but we’ll dig into that later.
Gwynne Shotwell is the real head of SpaceX&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.480
and she’s the only reason that SpaceX keeps&nbsp;
hitting all these insane milestones. But I’m not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.760
being an Elon hater here. Hiring a great operator&nbsp;
to help execute a massive vision is one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.160
best things that an entrepreneur can do. 
And Gwynne is seriously the best operator in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.640
the entire tech industry, but no one seems to&nbsp;
talk about her. I have a bunch of theories as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.960
to why that is, but let me show you&nbsp;
exactly how Gwynne runs SpaceX. 
&nbsp;

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:46.000
Elon is known for setting really ambitious&nbsp;
goals, but Gwynne is the person who actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.480
makes those dreams a reality. Lots of&nbsp;
founders hire “adult supervision” when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.640
their companies start to get really big. 
Mark Zuckerberg hired Sheryl Sandberg as his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:59.040
Chief Operating Officer in 2008, when the&nbsp;
company was just a few years old. Gwynne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.160
is Chief Operating Officer of SpaceX, but&nbsp;
she’s also the President of the company. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.080
that extra title is really meaningful. 
The title of President can mean a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.200
different things depending on the company,&nbsp;
but what I’m about to show you will make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.680
it clear that she’s much more than a second in&nbsp;
command. SpaceX would be nowhere without her.
&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.320
When Elon posts a crazy tweet suggesting&nbsp;
that a new SpaceX rocket might blow up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:26.480
it’s Gwynne Shotwell who flies all over the world&nbsp;
to assure key business partners that the launch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:31.200
will go according to plan. She’s a steading force&nbsp;
in the company, but that doesn’t mean she isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.880
100% aligned with Elon’s ambitions. She wants&nbsp;
to create a Mars colony as much as Elon does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.200
but unlike Elon, she has a financially viable&nbsp;
plan for how to actually get it done.
&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.120
I love analyzing leaders like Gwynne on this&nbsp;
channel because there are so many key lessons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.520
that can be applied to any startup. I’m an&nbsp;
entrepreneur myself and learning about success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.000
stories like hers always inspires me to work&nbsp;
harder and dream bigger. I put out videos like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.560
this weekly, so please consider subscribing.
Not much has been written about Gwynne Shotwell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.360
so I really had to dig through interviews and&nbsp;
podcasts to figure out what’s allowed her to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:09.200
scale SpaceX so effectively. The space industry&nbsp;
is highly regulated, extremely capital intensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.200
and just incredibly difficult to succeed in. It&nbsp;
is literally rocket science after all. But I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:18.400
I’ve nailed down how Gwynne thinks about business,&nbsp;
so let me summarize it in three separate parts.
&nbsp;

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:23.120
First up is her background. Before she&nbsp;
became President of SpaceX, Gwynne spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:27.760
decades becoming an expert in the relevant&nbsp;
skill sets needed to succeed in the aerospace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.240
industry. There are tons of startup ideas&nbsp;
that don’t require highly technical expertise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:36.560
just look at all the social networks founded in&nbsp;
dorm rooms by college dropouts that are now worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:41.360
billions. But rocketry is a little different. 
Now, Gwynne didn’t know that she wanted to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.480
in the aerospace industry as a kid. She’s actually&nbsp;
said that she never dreamed of being an astronaut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.400
which is kind of crazy since I thought&nbsp;
everyone had that dream as a kid. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.560
she had a fascination with engineering&nbsp;
and leaned into it at a young age.
&nbsp;

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:59.360
“I don’t think I was nerdy, but I was definitely&nbsp;
doing the things that the girls weren’t doing. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:04.000
asked my mom, who was an artist, when I was in the&nbsp;
third grade, how a car worked. She had no idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.840
so she gave me a book, and I read&nbsp;
it, and sure enough, my first job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.800
out of my mechanical engineering degree was with&nbsp;
Chrysler Motors in the automotive industry.”
&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:16.960
This is one of the best things about technical&nbsp;
disciplines. Not many people are willing to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:21.520
crack open a book and start learning. But if you&nbsp;
can buckle down and learn the science, you’ll have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.480
an incredible advantage over the competition.
But Elon Musk didn’t trust Gwynne to run SpaceX&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:31.040
just because she was a talented engineer. By&nbsp;
the time Gwynne joined SpaceX, she had worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:37.040
in the aerospace industry for over a decade. And&nbsp;
I can’t stress enough how important this is. 
&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:42.480
Many people think of themselves as their job&nbsp;
title. A typical engineer might bounce around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:48.160
from an advertising company, to a bank, and then&nbsp;
to a car company, and that can be a great career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:51.600
but if you want to become a leader like&nbsp;
Gwynne, it’s much better to focus on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:55.760
developing a breadth of skills that are&nbsp;
relevant to your specific industry.
&nbsp;

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.960
Instead of getting stuck in a particular&nbsp;
division of SpaceX when she joined,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:03.440
Gwynne was able to wear many hats because&nbsp;
she had so much aerospace experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:08.400
She came in as head of business development,&nbsp;
which is essentially a sales role, but because she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:14.320
understood the industry inside and out, Elon kept&nbsp;
tapping her for bigger and bigger projects. 
&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:18.800
It can be uncomfortable to take on new roles&nbsp;
at a growing company, but Gwynne understands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:23.200
that building a diverse skillset is key&nbsp;
to successful leadership, and you’ll never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.680
build new skills if you don’t take risks.
“When I was considering joining SpaceX back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:33.120
in 2002 I was struggling with the decision and&nbsp;
drawing it out for weeks. It seemed so risky for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:37.440
me personally to join this little startup in&nbsp;
an industry where none had ever succeeded. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:41.840
the time, I was a part time single mother, and&nbsp;
this was just too far out of my comfort zone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.320
I was driving on the freeway here in LA when&nbsp;
it finally hit me: I was being a total idiot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:51.280
Who cares if I tried this job and either I failed&nbsp;
or the company failed. What I recognized at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:56.160
that moment was that it was the trying part&nbsp;
that was the most important. Try that risky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.720
thing. Be a part of something exciting.”
When opportunity knocks, you need to open the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:06.240
door. Joining a startup as employee number seven&nbsp;
was extremely risky for Gwynne, but by taking a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:11.680
big leap of faith, she put herself in the perfect&nbsp;
position to expand her skill set and become a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.160
true leader in the aerospace industry. 
But all her ambition and experience would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:21.920
nothing without execution. This was nearly 20&nbsp;
years ago after all, and SpaceX had a long road&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:27.120
ahead of it. Becoming a successful rocket company&nbsp;
would require an entirely new way of thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:32.080
about the business, and fortunately for SpaceX,&nbsp;
Gwynne had discovered something incredible.
&nbsp;

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:36.480
If you’ve never heard the term “residual&nbsp;
capability” before, that’s completely fine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:39.840
It’s definitely pretty jargony, but it’s actually&nbsp;
quite simple in practice. Residual capability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:44.480
just means building profitable business&nbsp;
lines around technology you already have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:49.680
in order to support longer-term projects. And&nbsp;
this is Gywnne’s second key success factor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:53.920
She’s a master of capitalizing on&nbsp;
SpaceX’s residual capability.
&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:58.880
There are a few great examples of residual&nbsp;
capability really changing the game for SpaceX.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:04.400
The first is Starlink, their satellite internet&nbsp;
network. SpaceX didn’t set out to create an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:10.000
internet service provider. Their stated mission is&nbsp;
to make human life multi-planetary after all. 
&nbsp;

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:14.560
In fact, Elon Musk probably hadn’t thought about&nbsp;
providing internet service from space at all when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:19.520
started SpaceX. The internet is cool and all, but&nbsp;
it doesn’t have much to do with getting to Mars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:24.560
At least that’s what everyone thought, before&nbsp;
Gwynne created SpaceX’s financial plan.
&nbsp;

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:28.720
Getting to Mars wasn’t going to be cheap. The&nbsp;
rocket required to take a crew of people on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:33.920
the months-long journey will be absolutely&nbsp;
massive. So Gwynne started breaking Elon’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:37.920
vision down into small steps that could&nbsp;
ultimately lead to a Mars colony. 
&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.960
We’ve all seen SpaceX launch&nbsp;
satellites for other companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:45.280
but along the way Gwynne realized that by&nbsp;
launching their own satellites, they could create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:50.000
a sustainable business here on earth, and help&nbsp;
build technology that will be needed on Mars. 
&nbsp;

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.480
See, the cost of each incremental SpaceX launch&nbsp;
is decreasing over time. Every time they launch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:58.800
another rocket, they are learning important&nbsp;
lessons and becoming a more efficient company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:03.600
Launching a network of satellites into orbit&nbsp;
for Starlink would cost billions of dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:07.760
but once they were up there, they would&nbsp;
generate profits for decades to come.
&nbsp;

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:11.840
Additionally, a future Mars colony will&nbsp;
need a communications network of their own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:16.880
and all that experience setting up satellite&nbsp;
networks here on Earth will translate pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:21.280
directly into a Mars satellite network.&nbsp;
And Gwynne was the perfect person to lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:26.800
the Starlink effort at SpaceX, because she had&nbsp;
championed a deal with Iridium back in 2010. 
&nbsp;

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:31.760
SpaceX had great relationships with government&nbsp;
customers, but doing business with independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:38.080
corporations would be important too, so Gwynne&nbsp;
signed a $492 million dollar contract to launch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:42.000
75 Iridium satellites into&nbsp;
orbit using Falcon 9 rockets.
&nbsp;

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.080
Gwynne figured out how to bring in&nbsp;
extra business for SpaceX back in 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:51.840
and that experience allowed her to leverage that&nbsp;
“residual capability” to start an entirely new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:57.440
business line with Starlink in 2015. But that’s&nbsp;
not the only time that Gwynne has championed this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:02.240
idea of “residual capability” at SpaceX.
In fact, the entire design philosophy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:06.960
SpaceX’s Big Falcon Rocket is based&nbsp;
on the idea of Residual Capability:
&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:11.200
“It’s exactly this residual capability. So we&nbsp;
developed the Merlin engine for the Falcon 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:16.320
launch vehicle. We could have tossed that engine&nbsp;
and built an entirely new engine for the Falcon 9.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:19.920
It would have been called something different,&nbsp;
because Falcon 9 is nine Merlin engines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:24.560
but instead of spending a billion dollars on a&nbsp;
brand new engine, we put nine of them together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:29.200
on the back end of Falcon 9. Residual capability:&nbsp;
glue three Falcon 9s together and you have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:31.152
largest operational rocket flying. And so it&nbsp;
was expensive to do, but it was a much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.152 --> 00:08:33.680
efficient path than starting from scratch.”
And that’s not all, SpaceX is planning to offer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:38.320
point-to-point travel on Earth to allow&nbsp;
people to get from New York to Shanghai&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:43.440
in just 30 minutes. Developing this business&nbsp;
from scratch would be nearly impossible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:48.960
but because Gwynne had successfully executed&nbsp;
on so many other SpaceX projects, they now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.440
have a real shot at making this a reality,&nbsp;
although it probably won’t happen for years.
&nbsp;

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:56.560
Gwynne has been asked directly&nbsp;
about when this point-to-point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:00.560
capability would be widely available: 
“Within a decade, for sure. And this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:05.200
Gwynne time or Elon time? That's Gwynne time.&nbsp;
I'm sure Elon will want us to go faster.”
&nbsp;

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:08.720
This response tells you everything you&nbsp;
need to know about why Gwynne has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:14.240
so successful at SpaceX. Elon is notorious&nbsp;
for being overly optimistic about timelines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:18.640
and Gwynne acknowledges that while&nbsp;
still maintaining ambitious goals. 
&nbsp;

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:23.840
Too many people hear Elon announce an aggressive&nbsp;
timeline and instantly write him off because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:28.720
seems impossible. Gwynne understands that&nbsp;
Elon’s timelines might be overly ambitious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:33.040
but he’s still directionally correct about&nbsp;
where technology is going in the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:37.040
and she is 100% on board with his vision.
It’s common knowledge that Elon can be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:42.160
demanding boss, but Gwynne has been working&nbsp;
alongside Elon for nearly 20 years now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:46.320
and she wouldn’t stick around if they didn’t see&nbsp;
eye-to-eye on the future. And this is Gwynne’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:52.480
third key characteristic that makes her such a&nbsp;
great leader at SpaceX: She loves absurd goals:
&nbsp;

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:57.600
“Set and try to achieve absolutely absurd&nbsp;
goals and don’t be afraid of failing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:00.880
if you can’t achieve them”
This idea of setting absurd goals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:06.160
is extremely powerful, mostly because of human&nbsp;
psychology. See, this relates to something called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:11.840
Parkinson's Law, which is the old adage that&nbsp;
work expands to fill the time allotted. 
&nbsp;

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:19.120
To put it simply, the amount of work required&nbsp;
scales to the time available for its completion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:23.520
So if the SpaceX team said something like:&nbsp;
“getting to Mars will be really hard and will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:28.400
probably take 50 years” - they would almost&nbsp;
certainly spend 50 years on the project!
&nbsp;

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:32.400
But the downside risk of setting an&nbsp;
absurd goal is very minimal. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:37.440
people might make fun of you, but none of that&nbsp;
matters. Just keep building towards that goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:42.880
and you will eventually achieve great things. 
Elon is known to set particularly absurd goals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:47.920
and Gwynne is typically a bit more realistic&nbsp;
in her estimations. She has to keep the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:53.920
alive after all. But the key thing to note here&nbsp;
is that she is still 100% aligned with Elon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:59.120
on the goal. She has the same ability as Elon to&nbsp;
think long-term and imagine what’s possible for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:03.840
humanity over decades instead of just years. 
She’s made plenty of money at this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:08.720
but I think what keeps her going really is a&nbsp;
desire to improve the life of future generations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:14.560
She already has children and has said she wants&nbsp;
grandchildren. So even if SpaceX can’t accomplish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:19.360
every single goal within her lifetime, I think&nbsp;
she’ll be remembered for hundreds of years as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:23.680
someone who helped change the course of humanity&nbsp;
forever. And that’s incredibly powerful.
&nbsp;

